/**
\page native_procedural_animation Procedural jiggle and FABRIK

Use AnimationJiggle and AnimationFABRIK with Sprite's enabled animation pose,
or with an independent contiguous Part chain. The numeric solvers
solveAnimationJiggle(), stepJiggleTarget() and solveAnimationFABRIK() also work on
AnimationPose values without a Sprite. Springs use simulation seconds, frequency
in Hz, radians for angles and world engine units for displacement/acceleration.

For chain jiggle, AnimationJiggle::chain includes the root bone. Native Part
setJiggle() instead accepts following Parts separately; the receiver is the root.
Set a positive length for terminal independent Parts. AnimationJiggle::joints
provides sparse overrides identified by bone ID (stable Part ID for Part adapters).
Unspecified overrides inherit the controller's values. Native settings setters
accept a complete configuration; edit the value returned by get*JiggleOptions()
to preserve existing tuning. JavaScript setters accept sparse patches. Defaults and numeric
ranges are declared by AnimationJiggle and validated by validateAnimationJiggle().

IK-target jiggle requires an existing two-bone controller, identified by
AnimationJiggle::ik for Sprite. Filtering happens in world coordinates before the
IK solve. The stored desired target is unchanged. Physical Part IK drives accept
these filtered targets and continue to resolve contact through their existing
bodies and colliders. Chain jiggle does not create bodies or query obstacles.

FABRIK changes joint rotations, preserves offsets and endpoint orientation, and
returns AnimationFABRIKResult. At most maxIterations passes run, including for
unreachable targets. Sprite limits intersect with live Bone rotation limits.
Part solvers use live setIKLimits(). Part parent frames and local scales must be
uniform and nonsingular. Invalid configurations throw before editing output.

Modifiers run by stage, order, then registration. IK-target jiggle is a decorator;
chain jiggle is post-constraint. Built-in controllers reject competing ownership
of the same rotating joints. Springs keep numerical state separate from the
animated base, so repeated evaluation does not compound the effect. Reading
state or pose advances no simulation. Disabled springs freeze and reenabling
reseeds. set*Influence() can fade linearly in simulation seconds.

Full Sprite/Layer snapshots preserve native controllers, state, fades and Part
base rotations. Incremental records require unchanged topology. JiggleState
restores numerical state only; use full serialization to transfer configuration
and animated base motion together.

\par Native example
\code{.cpp}
pdg::AnimationJiggle settings;
settings.mode = pdg::jiggleMode_Chain;
settings.length = 12;
settings.frequency = 2;
settings.dampingRatio = 0.3;
root->setJiggle(settings, {middle, tip});
root->kickJiggle(2, 0, 1); // Add 2 radians/second to middle.
\endcode

\see pdg::Sprite::addAnimationJiggle(), pdg::Part::setJiggle(),
pdg::Sprite::addAnimationFABRIK(), pdg::Part::solveFABRIK()
*/

/**
\fn pdg::AnimationFABRIKResult pdg::solveAnimationFABRIK(AnimationPose &pose, const AnimationFABRIK &config, const AnimationTransform &root)
\brief Solve a contiguous pose chain with bounded FABRIK iterations.
\param pose Input and output pose; invalid inputs leave it unchanged.
\param config Root-to-endpoint chain, target, influence, tolerance and optional per-joint local limits. See \ref native_procedural_animation.
\param root Owning world transform; local targets use the first joint's parent frame.
\return Full-solve and applied endpoint error, convergence, iteration count, limit and geometric reach diagnostics.
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::validateAnimationJiggle(const AnimationJiggle &config)
\brief Validate finite spring tuning, ranges and sparse override identities.
\param config Jiggle configuration to check. Chain geometry is validated when solving.
\throws std::invalid_argument Invalid settings, duplicate overrides or overrides outside the chain.
*/

/**
\fn pdg::JiggleResult pdg::solveAnimationJiggle(AnimationPose &pose, const AnimationJiggle &config, JiggleState &state, double seconds, const AnimationTransform &root, const std::vector<double> &minimum, const std::vector<double> &maximum)
\brief Advance individual world-heading springs and apply chain rotations atomically.
\param pose Desired pose and resulting pose; local translations and scale are preserved.
\param config Chain mode configuration. Each joint needs a positive declared, inferred or overridden lever length.
\param state Persistent spring state; first evaluation seeds it from the desired pose.
\param seconds Nonnegative simulation seconds. Zero evaluates output without integrating.
\param root Owning world transform.
\param minimum Optional live local angle lower limits, one per chain joint.
\param maximum Matching upper limits; omit both arrays for no additional limits.
\return Applied influence, consumed time, substeps, and reset/limit diagnostics.
*/

/**
\fn pdg::JiggleResult pdg::stepJiggleTarget(const AnimationJiggle &config, JiggleState &state, double x, double y, double seconds)
\brief Advance a world-space target spring and compute the influenced target.
\param config IK-target jiggle tuning.
\param state Persistent target position and velocity; first evaluation seeds from the target.
\param x Desired world x coordinate.
\param y Desired world y coordinate.
\param seconds Nonnegative simulation seconds; zero does not integrate.
\return Desired, filtered and effective positions, lag, consumed time and work/reset diagnostics.
*/
